From bc8de0bf7cfec56a16c3eecd7cfcfcd88abaa2b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Philip Dye <phdye@acm.org>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 13:56:35 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] x86_64: reach the kernel through the gate and the thread
 pointer through %gs

Substrate N of elf-sysv-nt runs this glibc as ordinary NT threads in a
process that has no syscall trap and no FS base of its own: NT cannot trap
the syscall instruction, and it does not preserve a user-written FS base
across a context switch (spike 1). The kernel publishes a gate address in
the auxiliary vector as AT_SYSINFO, reached with a plain call under the
Linux register convention (%rcx and %r11 clobbered, everything else kept),
and keeps the thread pointer in TlsSlots[63] of the TEB, %gs:0x1678, with
the stack-protector canary at %gs:0x1670 and the pointer guard at
%gs:0x1668 (DR-0101). This port makes sysdeps say the same.

The gate. The address lives in GLRO(dl_sysinfo), as the i386 port keeps its
vsyscall entry (NEED_DL_SYSINFO, in a new x86_64 dl-sysdep.h); the default
is a stub that faults at a named symbol, since there is no instruction to
fall back on. Every entry goes through it the way its object reaches any
other GLRO word: _rtld_local_ro inside ld.so, the GOT elsewhere, _dl_sysinfo
in a static link. Nothing reads the TCB copy on the way in, because ld.so
maps and protects segments before it has a thread pointer and a static
program allocates its TLS with brk before it has one. sysdep.h carries the
assembler form (ENTER_KERNEL, %r11 as scratch) and the C form, which also
steps over the red zone, since the compiler cannot see the call. The
offset comes from a new rtld-global-offsets.sym, generated in csu like
ucontext_i.sym and guarded in sysdep.h against the .sym compile itself
(BZ #22792). The hand-written sites follow: syscall.S, clone.S, vfork.S,
lowlevellock.S, the three *context.S, ____longjmp_chk.S (whose sigaltstack
buffer sits in the red zone, so it steps over it), the errno stores in
sysdep.S, and __restore_rt in sigaction.c. vfork keeps its number: the
kernel's vfork is a fork the parent waits on, which is what the wrapper's
callers rely on.

The thread pointer. TLS_INIT_TP is a store to the slot, with no arch_prctl.
THREAD_SELF loads the slot; THREAD_GETMEM and its family are ordinary
accesses through the descriptor, the shape aarch64 and the other ports
without a segment base use, and the two callers that passed an
uninitialised descriptor (THREAD_DTV, INSTALL_NEW_DTV) now name the current
thread. THREAD_SET_STACK_GUARD and THREAD_SET_POINTER_GUARD write the TCB
copy as before and the TEB slot the compiler reads. A new thread's slots
start empty, so clone.S fills them from the TCB, where the creating thread
put the process's values, before the first protected frame. PTR_MANGLE
reads the pointer-guard slot. dl-tlsdesc.S, tls_get_addr.S and the four
strcmp variants that fetch the locale from TLS by hand take the pointer
from the slot into the register they were already loading.

What is replaced rather than ported. The x86_64 cancellation.S and its two
includes are removed in favour of nptl's C versions, which the tls.h
macros already serve: the assembler kept to a register convention that
left no register for the descriptor. The catomic_* forms in
atomic-machine.h, which tested the TCB's multiple_threads word to skip
the lock prefix, lock unconditionally, which is include/atomic.h's fallback
for a port that defines none; malloc and dl-profile are the users.

Untouched. The CET paths (SHSTK_ENABLED, feature_1 and ssp_base through
%fs) are compiled out without --enable-cet and stay as upstream wrote
them. libthread_db's DB_THREAD_SELF still names the FS register, which is
recorded as unverified. The x32 subdirectory is not built.

The three slot constants are defined once, in tls.h, for C and assembler.
The post-link check in toolchain/glibc/check-no-syscall is what holds the
result: no syscall instruction and no %fs access in any object produced.
